-- title:
--   Prime-norm elements of a quaternion order lie in the Hecke set at ℓ
-- statement:
--   Let $q,q'$ be primes, let $a,b \in \mathbb{Q}$ and let $B = \mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ be the associated quaternion algebra, assumed to satisfy `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt`, i.e. $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ the completed algebra $B \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ has all nonzero elements invertible exactly when $q$ or $q'$ lies in $v$. Let $\Lambda \subseteq B$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule that is a maximal order, meaning it contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $B$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, is finitely generated, and is the only order containing it. Let $\ell$ be a prime and $x \in \Lambda$ with reduced norm $\mathrm{nrd}(x) = x_{\mathrm{re}}^2 - a x_{\mathrm{imI}}^2 - b x_{\mathrm{imJ}}^2 + ab\, x_{\mathrm{imK}}^2$ equal to $\ell$. The conclusion is that there is a unit $h$ of $B \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ belonging to `primeHeckeSet` $\Lambda$ $\ell$ — that is, $h$ lies in the additive subgroup generated by the elements $z \otimes \alpha$ with $z \in \Lambda$ and $\alpha$ a finite adele integral at every place, likewise $\ell \cdot h^{-1}$ lies in that subgroup, while $h^{-1}$ and $\ell^{-1} h$ do not — whose underlying element is the principal adele $x \otimes 1$. The proof uses neither the hypothesis `hB` nor the maximality clause of `hΛ`, only that $\Lambda$ is an order.
--
--   This identifies global elements of reduced norm $\ell$ in a quaternion order with points of the Hecke set at $\ell$ used in the Hecke correspondence on the associated quaternionic Shimura curve. It serves as the bridge from an explicit element of prime norm to the adelic Hecke condition, and is used in the level-one statement on period maps for fake elliptic curves in the Čerednik–Drinfeld setting.
